-------------------------------------- Remember me --------------------------------------------------

- HAVING TO LOG IN EVERY TIME IS PAINFUL: LET'S REMEMBER OUR USER FOR THE NEXT TIME! PLAY WITH THE
    LOCALSTORAGE AND EFFECTS.

Instruction: 

- ⚠️You have probably noticed that every time we reload the page, we lose the logged in user.
    Indeed, every time the page is reloaded, the application restarts from scratch, and the
    UserService is thus recreated in its initial state. Let’s improve that.

1️⃣To be able to remember the logged-in user, we need to store it in a persistent location, and not
    just in memory. The browser has an API to do that: LocalStorage. So, every time the user logs
    in, we will store the user in LocalStorage. When the application starts (i.e. when we load or
    reload the page), we will also read the logged-in user from LocalStorage and expose it in
    UserService.

        constructor() {
        
        }


2️⃣ We can handle the synchronization with the LocalStorage using an effect. Every time the value of
    the signal containing the current user changes, we want to update the LocalStorage. This is 
    xactly what an effect() is for. Add an effect() in the constructor of the service to update the
    localStorage every time the value of the signal changes (by calling setItem), or to remove the
    value from the localStorage if the user is undefined (by calling removeItem).

        constructor() {
            effect(() => {
                // every time the user signal changes, we store it in local storage
                const user = this.user();
                untracked(() => {
                    if (user) {
                        window.localStorage.setItem(USER_LOCAL_STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(user));
                    } else {
                        window.localStorage.removeItem(USER_LOCAL_STORAGE_KEY);
                    }
                });
            })
        }

    LocalStorage is accessible using window.localStorage. It has a method setItem(key, value). 🔥The
    key must be a string. Use rememberMe. 🔥Note that the value must also be a string. You can thus
    serialize the user to JSON using JSON.stringify(user), and store the result.


3️⃣ The second phase consists in reading the stored value at startup. Add a new private method
    retrieveUser(). This method should read, deserialize the user (with JSON.parse()) from LocalStorage
    (if it exists), and return it. It should return undefined if the user is not stored in
    LocalStorage. Then use this method as the initial value of the user signal.

        private readonly user = signal<UserModel | undefined>(this.retrieveUser());
        
        // omitted

        private retrieveUser(): UserModel | undefined {
            const value = window.localStorage.getItem(USER_LOCAL_STORAGE_KEY);
            if (value) {
                return JSON.parse(value) as UserModel;
            }
            return undefined;
        }


